  "query_preview": "(This evening through Thursday)...\nIssued at 149 PM EDT Thu Sep 24 2026\n\nREST OF TODAY AND TONIGHT\n\nThe overall synoptic setup remains similar \nover the area with a surface high over SE Canada and an upper low \njust to the SE. Cold, moist advection along the cold conveyor belt \nalong with modest subsidence has kept overcast skies over the Ohio \nValley for much of the day. There is some mid level dry air working \nits way into the area this afternoon which has allowed for some \nmixing and pockets of clearing. That said, broad clearing likely \nwont arrive until late this evening into the overnight as dry air \nentrainment increases on the northern end of the upper low occlusion \nalong with high pressure building in from the NE. Clearing will be a \nslow process, but should gradually move through central between \n02Z and 08Z from east to west. \n\nTemperatures overnight will have a modest gradient across the area \ndue to this temporal difference in clearing. Some areas over far NE \ncentral Indiana where clearing is expected to begin may have enough \ndiurnal cooling to reach the mid 40s by day break. The rest of the \narea should see lows near to just above 50. Some models are \nmaintaining cloud cover over the southern Wabash Valley as pressure \ngains wane with the low moving off to the east. If this occurs, lows \nmay only briefly fall below 60 over W/SW portions of the area.  I \nSurface high pressure, centered over southeastern Canada, has be\n\nFRIDAY THROUGH SUNDAY\n\nFor tomorrow, a new broad area of low pressure begins to develop \nover the Mississippi Valley leading to an additional source of cloud \ncover over central Indiana. Low clouds are likely to be much less \nabundant, but upper level cirrus will likely push through the area \nthroughout the day leading to opaque, mostly cloudy skies.  There is \nsome uncertainty with how thick the cirrus shield will be, and \ntherefor its impact on high temperatures, generally the expectation \nis for highs in the low 70s, but areas with thinner cirrus may push \ninto the mid 70s tomorrow afternoon. \n\nHigh pressure will continue to build within easterly flow tomorrow \ninto the weekend, creating greater subsidence over the area. This \nshould keep the broad region of low pressure to the west, \neventually resulting in mostly clear skies over the weekend once \ncirrus debris leaves the area. Diurnal cumulus is likely over \nwestern portions of the area, with gradual temperature gains each \nday. This will lead to a very seasonal late September weekend \nacross the area. Greater diurnal cooling along with lingering \nsurface moisture Friday night may lead to patchy dense fog, but \nuncertainty is too high to include at this time. \n\nNEXT WEEK\n\nGuidance is beginning to hint at a potent system developing over the \nPlains around the Tuesday timeframe. Current ensemble suite \ngenerally brings the low center to our northwest, with the trailing \ncold front passing through Indiana. Uncertainty remains regarding \nthe strength and track of the low, but enough of a signal is present \nto justify higher PoPs Tuesday night through early Thursday. \nThunderstorms are also possible, but deep warm moist advection \nappears limited despite the system's potential strength. For now, \nmost members of guidance show only low amounts of MLCAPE (under 500 \nJ/Kg). This system resembles a cold-season synoptic low with minimal \ninstability and high shear. So, despite the low MLCAPE, this system \nstill bears watching from a severe weather standpoint.",
